## bo-body | The human body | bio | 0 | - | bi-anim
explain 3 | Your stomach lining replaces itself every few days
- Stomach acid has a pH around 1.5–3.5: strong enough to dissolve some metals.
- A protective mucus layer and fast cell turnover stop it digesting you.
- *Barry Marshall drank a culture of H. pylori in 1984 to prove bacteria cause ulcers. Nobel Prize, 2005.*
> Your blood vessels could circle the Earth

quiz 3 | Laid end to end, roughly how long are an adult's blood vessels?
* About 100,000 km
o About 1,000 km
o About 10 km
o About 1 million km
= Most of it is capillaries, each thinner than a hair. Estimates vary, but around 100,000 km is often cited.
= That's about two and a half times around the Earth.
> Bones are stronger than steel, by weight

explain 3 | Bone: lightweight and self-repairing
- Bone's strength-to-weight ratio rivals some steels, and unlike steel it repairs itself.
- Your skeleton is gradually remodelled throughout life; estimates suggest roughly 10% a year in adults.
- *Astronauts lose bone mass in microgravity because bones respond to load.*
> You're taller in the morning

real 3 | You shrink during the day
- The discs between your vertebrae compress while you're upright.
- Most people are about 1 cm taller in the morning.
- *Astronauts in orbit grow up to about 3% taller, then shrink back on Earth.*
> Your nose can detect many smells

explain 4 | How many smells can you detect?
- A 2014 study claimed humans can distinguish over a trillion odours.
- Other researchers disputed the maths; the true number is uncertain.
- *Either way, the old "10,000 smells" figure had no solid basis.*
> Next: nutrition myths

## bo-food | Nutrition myths | bio | 0 | - | bo-body
myth 3 F | Sugar makes children hyperactive.
= Double-blind studies have repeatedly found no effect of sugar on children's behaviour.
= Parents who were told their child had sugar rated them as more hyperactive, even when they'd had none.
> You don't need 8 glasses of water a day

myth 3 F | Everyone needs to drink eight glasses of water a day on top of everything else.
= Food and other drinks provide much of your water. Needs vary with size, activity and climate.
= Thirst is a decent guide for most healthy people.
> Breakfast and metabolism

explain 4 | Is breakfast the most important meal?
- The phrase was popularised by cereal marketing in the early 1900s.
- Randomised trials haven't shown that eating breakfast causes weight loss.
- *Eat it if it suits you. It isn't magic.*
> Calories in, calories out, mostly

explain 4 | A calorie is a unit of energy
- A food calorie (kcal) is the energy to heat 1 kg of water by 1°C.
- Bomb calorimeters burn food to measure it; digestion extracts less from some foods, like nuts.
- *Weight change follows energy balance, but hunger and metabolism respond, which makes it hard.*
> Next: exercise science

## bo-ex | Exercise science | bio | 0 | - | bo-food
explain 4 | Exercise grows new brain cells (in animals) and helps memory
- Aerobic exercise increases BDNF, a protein that supports neuron growth.
- Studies in older adults found a year of walking enlarged the hippocampus slightly.
- *It's one of the best-supported ways to protect memory.*
> Muscle soreness isn't lactic acid

myth 3 F | The muscle soreness you feel a day or two after exercise is caused by lactic acid build-up.
= Lactate clears within about an hour after exercise.
= Delayed-onset muscle soreness comes from microscopic damage and the inflammation that repairs it.
> VO₂ max

explain 4 | VO₂ max: your aerobic engine size
- The maximum rate your body can use oxygen, in ml per kg per minute.
- Untrained adults: around 35–45. Elite endurance athletes: 70–90.
- *Higher VO₂ max is strongly associated with longer life.*
> Next: genetics fun

## bo-gene | Genetics oddities | bio | 0 | - | bo-ex
explain 3 | Some people can taste a chemical others can't
- PTC tastes intensely bitter to some people and like nothing to others.
- The difference is largely down to one gene, TAS2R38.
- *It was discovered by accident in 1931 when a chemist's lab partner complained about the bitter dust.*
> Coriander tastes of soap to some

explain 3 | Why coriander tastes like soap to some people
- A variant near olfactory receptor genes, including OR6A2, is linked to detecting soapy-smelling aldehydes in coriander.
- Rates vary by population, from a few percent to over a fifth.
- *It's partly genetic, partly familiarity.*
> We're all a bit Neanderthal

quiz 3 | Roughly how much Neanderthal DNA do people of non-African descent carry?
* About 1–2%
o About 25%
o About 0.0001%
o About 10%
= Interbreeding happened as modern humans spread out of Africa.
= Some of those genes affect immunity and skin.
> Next: evolution oddities

## bo-evo | Evolution's odd designs | bio | 0 | - | bo-gene
explain 4 | The recurrent laryngeal nerve takes a detour
- It runs from the brain, down into the chest, loops under a major blood vessel, and back up to the larynx.
- In giraffes, that's a detour of several metres.
- *Evolution tinkers with what exists: in fish ancestors the route was direct.*
> Your eyes are wired backwards

explain 4 | The vertebrate eye is wired inside out
- Light passes through nerve fibres and blood vessels before reaching photoreceptors.
- That's what creates the blind spot.
- *Octopus eyes evolved separately and have the wiring behind the receptors: no blind spot.*
> Hiccups may be a fishy leftover

explain 3 | Hiccups might be an evolutionary leftover
- One hypothesis links hiccups to the reflex tadpoles use to push water over their gills while blocking the airway.
- It's speculative, but the nerve pathways involved are ancient.
- *The record for continuous hiccups: about 68 years (Charles Osborne).*
> Body track complete

## en-eng | Engineering marvels | physics | 0 | - | sp-miss
story 4 | The Eiffel Tower grows in summer
- Thermal expansion makes the iron tower around 15 cm taller on hot days.
- It was meant to be temporary, built for the 1889 World's Fair, and survived partly as a radio antenna.
- *Many Parisian artists signed a petition against it before it was built.*
> Bridges

explain 4 | Why suspension bridges can be so long
- Cables in pure tension carry the deck's weight to tall towers and anchorages.
- Steel is far stronger in tension than stone is in compression over long spans.
- *The longest spans exceed 2 km (Çanakkale, Turkey, 2022).*
> The Burj Khalifa

quiz 3 | How tall is the Burj Khalifa?
* About 828 m
o About 541 m
o About 1,000 m
o About 452 m
= Completed 2010. Its Y-shaped plan helps disrupt wind vortices.
> The Panama Canal lifts ships over a continent

explain 4 | The Panama Canal is a water elevator
- Locks raise ships about 26 m to Gatun Lake, then lower them on the other side.
- Each transit uses around 200 million litres of fresh water from the lake.
- *Droughts in 2023 forced limits on how many ships could pass.*
> Engineering track complete

## ev-phys | Everyday physics 2 | physics | 0 | - | en-eng
explain 3 | How a microwave oven heats food
- Microwaves at about 2.45 GHz make water molecules rotate back and forth, generating heat.
- Food heats unevenly because of standing-wave hot spots: that's why there's a turntable.
- *Percy Spencer noticed a chocolate bar melting in his pocket near a radar magnetron in 1945.*
> Rainbows are cones

explain 4 | A rainbow is a circle centred opposite the Sun
- Sunlight refracts into raindrops, reflects off the back, and exits at about 42° for red, 40° for violet.
- Every observer sees their own rainbow from different drops.
- *From a plane you can sometimes see the full circle.*
> Why ice is slippery

explain 4 | Why ice is slippery is still debated
- The old explanation, pressure melting, doesn't produce enough melting at normal temperatures.
- Current explanations focus on a thin, liquid-like layer on the ice surface, and friction heating.
- *A surprisingly unsettled question for something so familiar.*
> Why a spinning football curves

explain 4 | The Magnus effect
- A spinning ball drags air around with it, deflecting the airflow and pushing the ball sideways.
- Roberto Carlos's 1997 free kick curved dramatically around a wall.
- *Tennis topspin dives for the same reason.*
> Why the sea is blue and salty

explain 3 | Why is the sea salty?
- Rain slowly weathers rocks, carrying dissolved minerals to the sea.
- Water evaporates; the salt stays.
- *About 3.5% salt by mass on average.*
> Everyday physics part two complete
